Qwen2.5-72B & 3.02 & 2.94 & $+1.17$ & $+1.18$ \\
Qwen3-8B & 1.11 & 1.15 & $+0.53$ & $+0.52$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Posterior estimates under Horseshoe (HS) and HalfNormal (HN) contamination priors. Both priors produce virtually identical estimates: Pearson $r = 1.000$ for $\hat{\theta}$ and $r = 0.9999$ for $\hat{c}_n$.}
\label{tab:prior_robustness}
\end{table}

Posterior estimates are virtually identical across the two prior specifications (Pearson $r = 1.000$ for $\hat{\theta}$, $r > 0.999$ for $\hat{c}_n$), indicating that results are not sensitive to the choice of contamination prior.


\section{Item-Level Fit Diagnostics}
\label{app:item_diagnostics}

We report item-level infit and outfit MNSQ statistics for all 176 items. Items with MNSQ outside $[0.5, 1.5]$ are flagged as potentially misfitting.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Statistic} & \textbf{Mean} & \textbf{SD} & \textbf{Median} & \textbf{Min} & \textbf{Max} \\
\midrule
Infit MNSQ & 0.78 & 0.41 & 0.76 & 0.05 & 1.95 \\
Outfit MNSQ & 0.73 & 0.57 & 0.58 & 0.04 & 3.17 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Item-level fit statistics across 176 items.}
\label{tab:item_fit_summary}
\end{table}

Of the 176 items, 92 are flagged: 18 overfit (MNSQ $> 1.5$) and 74 underfit (MNSQ $< 0.5$). The majority of flagged items are underfit, reflecting sparse low-observation items where the model's predicted variance exceeds the observed variance---a consequence of limited data per item rather than a structural model deficiency. The 18 overfit items exhibit more erratic response patterns than the model predicts. Importantly, no model-level or judge-level facets are flagged (all MNSQ within $[0.63, 1.07]$), confirming that the G-MFRM captures the systematic structure in model ability, judge severity, and contamination.


\section{CI Coverage Analysis}
\label{app:ci_coverage}

We assess empirical coverage of 95\% posterior credible intervals across synthetic replications. At $N=8$ (49 seeds), ability parameter $\theta$ achieves 90.1\% coverage, approaching the nominal level, while contamination susceptibility $c_n$ achieves only 67.9\%. At $N=50$ (50 seeds; exp-009), $c_n$ coverage improves to 71\%. The coverage gap between $\theta$ and $c_n$ reflects the multiplicative coupling $c_n \cdot e_i$: contamination parameters enter the likelihood through an interaction term, making posterior inference more sensitive to exposure estimation noise and the HalfNormal prior's boundary constraint at $c_n = 0$. These results support treating $c_n$ point estimates and rank orderings as reliable diagnostics, while noting that $c_n$ credible intervals are systematically too narrow.

\section{Bias vs.\ Confounding Level}
\label{app:bias_vs_rho}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_bias_vs_rho}
\caption{Sequential estimator bias remains approximately constant across confounding levels ($\rho$), confirming omission-driven rather than confounding-strength-dependent bias. Joint G-MFRM estimation reduces bias by approximately 70\% at all $\rho$ levels. Synthetic data: $N = 50$, $I = 100$, $J = 8$.}
\label{fig:bias_vs_rho_app}
\end{figure}


\section{Error Propagation Details}
\label{app:error_prop}

\begin{table}[h]
